@startuml usage2_ci_loop
' What running GOPAL in CI actually looks like.
'
' Diagram 1 explains where the inputs come from. This one shows the loop they
' sit in, because "policy as code" only means something once a policy can fail
' a pull request the way a unit test does.
!include gopal-style.puml

title GOPAL as a required status check

|#F8FAFC|Repository|
start
:Pull request opened;
:Compliance facts committed\nalongside the code\n//contract.json//;

|#F1EBFA|Evaluation|
:AICertify runs the evaluators\nDeepEval, LangFair, or your own;
:Metrics written into the contract\n//metrics.content_safety.score//\n//metrics.fairness.gender_bias//;

|#EEF2FF|Policy|
:Pin a GOPAL bundle\n//gopal-international-eu_ai_act-1.3.1.tar.gz//;
:opa eval against the contract;

if (Every policy satisfied?) then (yes)
  |#F8FAFC|Repository|
  #E6F4F1:Status check passes\nMerge allowed;
  |#FEF3C7|Evidence|
  :Report retained as a build artefact\nPDF, Markdown or JSON;
  stop
else (no, or no conclusion)
  |#F8FAFC|Repository|
  #FEE4E2:Status check fails\nnaming the article and the control;
  :Fix the system, or record the fact,\nor challenge the policy;
  stop
endif

@enduml
